## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design scalable feature flags with per-user rollout, regional controls, and safe hot flips.

## Key Concepts
- Global distribution and eventual consistency
- Data modeling for flags and segments
- Cache invalidation and TTL
- Rollout semantics and hot-flip safety

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluateFeature(user, flag, context){
  const bucket = hash(user.id + flag.name) % 100;
  const inSegment = (flag.segments && flag.segments.length) ? flag.segments.includes(user.segment) : true;
  const regionOK = flag.regionOverride ? user.region === flag.regionOverride : true;
  return (bucket < flag.rolloutPct) && inSegment && regionOK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate rollout accuracy under traffic spikes?
- How would you rollback a faulty flag without service interruption?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical array manipulation skills while maintaining sorted order, a common frontend data processing task. It evaluates understanding of efficient algorithms and the ability to work with constraints (no mutation, sorted inputs).

## Key Concepts
- Two-pointer technique for merging sorted arrays
- Deduplication by comparing with the last added element
- Time and space complexity analysis
- Non-mutating operations

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeUnique(a, b) {
  let i = 0, j = 0, result = [];

  while (i < a.length || j < b.length) {
    let current;

    if (j >= b.length || (i < a.length && a[i] <= b[j])) {
      current = a[i++];
    } else {
      current = b[j++];
    }

    if (result.length === 0 || result[result.length - 1] !== current) {
      result.push(current);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this if inputs might not be sorted?
- What if we need to preserve the original order of first occurrence?
- How would this change for very large datasets that don't fit in memory?
- Could we optimize further if we know the range of possible tags?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests diagnosing certificate rotation within a service mesh, focusing on end-to-end TLS verification, per-tenant isolation, and zero-downtime deployment strategies.

## Key Concepts

- TLS certificate rotation propagation across Kubernetes secrets and Envoy proxies
- mTLS handshake troubleshooting in Istio service mesh
- Canary rollouts with automated rollback capabilities
- Per-tenant routing and isolation in multi-tenant mesh environments

## Code Example

```javascript
// TLS handshake tester (pseudo)
async function testTLS(target) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(target);
    return response.ok;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

